{:edb/db-before-t 830 :edb/db-after-t 831 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534143 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:10:50.390Z" 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186162064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412049 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186162066 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412050 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186162285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158912 13194139534143 17592186045417 false] [17592186162285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412047 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186162285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186162285 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186162285 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = λ_{2} = 4$ and $λ_{3} =-2$. Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139534143 17592186045417 false] [17592186162285 1074 "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block ${J}_{1}(-2)$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =4:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block ${J}_{2}(4).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely ${J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(4).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =4.$\n\nComputing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A-4I)=1,$ there will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4,$ say, ${v}_{1}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{2}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412030 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412051 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412030 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412052 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412030 1043 #edb/