39534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412049 1072 #uuid "48d72277-36e5-5416-8dca-632269341756" 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412049 1074 "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda =7$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda =5$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block ${J}_{1}(5)$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =7:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block ${J}_{2}(7).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely ${J}_{1}(7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(7).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =7.$\n\nComputing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=2,$ there will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$ say, ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A-7I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(5)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412048 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412050 1069 "q-110376" 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412050 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-7.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412050 1072 #uuid "c099d5a5-3b8f-5bcf-9f18-6240f88f8abb" 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412050 1074 "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(5)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-7)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139534143 17592186045417 true] [17592186412051 1058 #edb/ref 17592186412053 13194139534143 17592186045417 